ling of charge noise from individual electrostatic \ufb02uctu- ations is developed and described in the next section. FIG. 7. Exchange interaction vs tunnel gate voltage calcu- lated for DQD devices with di\ufb00erent inter-dot distances. The plotted values are calculated using the full con\ufb01guration in- teraction model. III. NOISE THEORETICAL DESCRIPTION In the context of semiconductor spin qubits, charge noise is essentially the electrostatic \ufb02uctuations in a quantum dot\u2019s environment. Such \ufb02uctuations modu- late the quantum dot\u2019s con\ufb01nement potential and hence the electron wave functions. In the presence of any spin to charge coupling mechanism, charge noise causes de- coherence in the spin qubit and induces quantum gate errors. To represent charge noise, we adopt a general dis- tributed trapping model [25] to describe the 1/f spectra. This approach describes charge noise as the collective ef- fect of many individual two-level \ufb02uctuations caused by the hopping of charges around a device. The sources of such \ufb02uctuations are referred to as Two-Level Fluctua- tors (TLFs). We present a statistical description of the charge noise in Sec.III A and III B. We then examine the microscopic description of the electrostatics of the noise sources in Sec.III C A. Two-Level Fluctuators (TLFs) The signature e\ufb00ect of a TLF is observed as a bi-stable signal known as a Random Telegraph Signal (RTS) shown in Fig.8. The number of switching events in a RTS is described by a Poisson process and it can be charac- terized by three main parameters: the amplitude of the signal(\u2206\u00b5), the average time spent in the high level (\u03c41) and in the low level (\u03c40). The power spectral density of a RTS is a Lorentzian as shown in the inset of Fig.8 and is mathematically described by the formula [24]"
