osition of one of the wells and rl is the separation between the two wells among which the charge is alternating. A \ufb02uctuating dipole with both a positive and a negative charge would cause a electrostatic potential \ufb02uctuation two times the one of a single charge moving between two wells (Udip = 2 UDWP ). Furthermore, a commonly used [54\u201356] description of the electrostatics of a TLF is one of a whole charge trap (Fig.10(a)) screened by the 2DEG in the reservoirs and in the Thomas-Fermi approximation is given by the formula UWHS(r) = e2 4\u03c0\u01eb0\u01ebr((x \u2212Xt)2 + (y \u2212Yt)2)3/2 (1 + qT F d qT F 2 ), (11) where qT F = 2/aB is the Thomas-Fermi wave vector with aB \u22483 nm; the e\ufb00ective Bohr radius in silicon and d is the depth of the trap in the oxide [57]. The expression in Eq.11 is derived under an approximation assuming large |r \u2212rt|. Hence, from the point of reference of a quantum dot sensing such \ufb02uctuations, a screened potential implies that such switching events are occurring further outside the perimeter of the dot (> 50 nm from its center) for them to be screened by the 2DEG. IV. NOISE SIMULATIONS In the context of studying spin qubits in quantum dots, charge noise is often introduced as dephasing, tunnelling or detuning noise [16]. The noise signal is either described as A/f or by a RTS (Eq.7,8) [55]. The amplitude of a sin- gle RTS is usually calculated within the Thomas-Fermi screening approximation (Eq.11) under the assumption of depleted quantum dot region where the charge impu- rities are only active above the electron reservoirs which provide the screening [54\u201356]. Charge sensing techniques have been widely adopted to characterize charge noise in quantum dot devices and spin qubit environments. In Ref.[23], a match is observed between the spectra extracted from qubit noise spec- troscopy and from charge sensing methods con\ufb01rming a common noise environment between the qubit and charge sensing quantu