lations with the simulations presented in Sec.V. However, in this appendix we study the correlation between noise spectral densities and quantum gate performance by adopting a simpler approach for calculating quantum gate \ufb01delity. 100 di\ufb00erent random distributions (spatially and temporally) of DWPs are created at a density of 1011cm\u22122. Fig.23 shows the mean, minimum and maximum of the calculated spectral densities with an inset showing the distribution of the spectral densities at 1Hz. Fig.23(a) describes the electrostatic potential noise (N) as described in Sec.IV while Fig.23(b) describes the noise on the qubit frequency (NQ) calculated using the electric \ufb01eld due to the TLFs as described in Sec.V A. We calculate average X-gate \ufb01delities over 1000 gate operations per TLF distribution. However, we assume that switching events of TLFs occur only between (not during) gate operations which allows for an analytic calculation of the \ufb01delity. In other words, we assume a constant frequency shift throughout each X- gate operation which can change from operation to another according to the TLFs switching frequencies. Single qubits gates are sensitive to the qubit frequency shifts which in turn are dependent on the electric \ufb01elds from TLFs. Such electric \ufb01eld dependence introduces a third variability factor when studying correlations between"
